#{[17592186311607 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311607 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335488" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311607 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311607 #edb/ref 1071 "A linear system of ODEs has an equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}.$ One of the nullclines of the system has the equation $y=-4x+1$ in the corresponding phase space. The system is then shifted using $u=x-{x}^{∗}.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be a nullcline of the shifted system?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311607 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b8143e4f-528e-5eef-b81d-03c2d102e474" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311607 #edb/ref 1074 "After shifting a system, the geometric structure of the vector field is preserved. In particular, each nullcline is translated but retains its orientation, meaning its slope does not change.\n\nBecause the equilibrium is moved to the origin by the shift, the translated nullclines must pass through the origin in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, the nullclines of the shifted system pass through the origin and have the same slopes as the corresponding nullclines of the original system.\n\nAmong the given options, the only line that satisfies the above conditions is $y=-4x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311610 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311610 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335522" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311610 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311610 #edb/ref 1071 "A nonlinear system of ODEs has exactly two equilibrium points. The system is then shifted using the transformation $u=x-{x}^{∗},$ where ${x}^{∗}$ is one of the equilibria. Which of the following statements about the number of equilibria in the shifted system is guaranteed to be true?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311610 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a9156c54-a0f8-59e7-b01b-764ac89d4134" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311610 #edb/ref 1074 "A shift of coordinates is a translation of the phase space and does not alter the structure of the system’s equilibrium set. Each equilibrium point of the original system is translated to a corresponding equilibrium in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, although one of the equilibria is moved to the origin, the total number of equilibrium points remains the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"The shifted system still has exactly two equilibrium points.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431869 #edb/ref 1080 "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+10 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+v(t)-3\\end{aligned}\\right." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186431869 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431869 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5760a8e4-95a8-56ad-9ea9-5dd8de11365f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431871 #edb/ref 1080 "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186431871 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431871 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6e795028-7b52-57a5-b776-1234735687ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431874 #edb/ref 1080 "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186431874 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431874 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "898f435b-7b14-5b2f-ae7d-4bcad320ae8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431877 #edb/ref 1080 "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186431877 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431877 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ae08dcf3-62ce-5a52-845e-1dd4af782784" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431883 #edb/ref 1080 "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3{u}^{2}(t)-{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186431883 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431883 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f0064606-8a67-5c4c-866a-5588c435a49b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431885 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431885 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431885 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431885 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431885 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431885 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431885 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431885 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186431874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431885 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "11f2b70f-bcb2-5554-a4f5-52982774bf66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431889 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431889 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335386" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431889 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431889 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+6y(t)-12 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)+2x(t)+y(t)-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,3]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186431889 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a1db4f53-42d8-55c9-8ba8-b7dde90ef735" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431889 #edb/ref 1074 "To shift the system’s equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+3,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+6y(t)-12 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =3(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+3)^{2}+6(v(t)+3)-12 \\\\  & =3u(t)+3-({v}^{2}(t)+6v(t)+9)+6v(t)+18-12 \\\\  & =3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+2x(t)+y(t)-6 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+1)^{2}+2(u(t)+1)+(v(t)+3)-6 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+1)+2u(t)+2+v(t)+3-6 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443]}
